    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d77">

                <pb facs="0179" n="69" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg69"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/1872: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Nanking</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-25T19:00:00-04:00">September 25, 1931—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>79. Your 68, September 25, 10 a.m. What the Secretary said, on the afternoon of
                    September 22, to the Japanese Ambassador<anchor n="70" xml:id="d77-f1"/> in this
                    connection was: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“It would seem that the responsibility for determining the course of
                            events with regard to the liquidating of this situation rests largely
                            upon Japan, for the simple reason that Japanese armed forces have seized
                            and are exercising <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> control in
                            South Manchuria.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>You may discreetly inform the inquirer by oral paraphrase.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
